WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:16.299
GMAIL’S POPULARITY:
Gmail’s popularity is falling off a cliff.

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:21.590
In September of 2021, they controlled 35.25%
of the market.

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:27.420
But by October of 2022, they only controlled
27.95% of the market.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:34.309
This means that within these 13 months, Gmail’s
market share fell by a whopping 7.3%.

00:00:34.309 --> 00:00:39.110
This might not seem like a large amount at
first glance, but it is when you consider

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:42.530
that basically everyone with an internet connection
uses email.

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:47.559
There’s estimated to be about 4.26 billion
email users worldwide.

00:00:47.559 --> 00:00:53.710
So, this means that Gmail either lost 306
million users or the people who are opening

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:57.649
new email accounts are choosing the other
email clients by far.

00:00:57.649 --> 00:01:02.670
It’s probably some sort of combination of
both, but why?

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:08.330
Ever since gmail launched, the consensus has
been that gmail is the go to email client.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:13.400
Whether we’re talking about personal use,
business use, or fake email use, gmail has

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.400
been the bomb.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.650
I would even argue that some of the other
email clients have had a negative stigma surrounding

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:21.159
them especially yahoo and hotmail.

00:01:21.159 --> 00:01:26.250
So, how did Gmail go from being the go to
email service to having losing a fifth of

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:29.180
their market share within a year.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:34.259
THE APPEAL OF GMAIL:
To understand why Gmail is losing so much

00:01:34.259 --> 00:01:39.299
market share, we’ll first have to understand
why they were so popular in the first place.

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:43.540
And for this, we’ll have to go back to April
fools day of 2004.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:47.840
If you didn’t know, Gmail was actually launched
as kind of a joke.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.150
In fact, Sundar Pichai, the current CEO of
Google, was interviewing with Google at the

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:53.150
time.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:57.380
And when interviewers asked him about his
thoughts on Gmail, even he thought that it

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.430
was just an April Fool’s Day joke.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:03.860
The main reason that this theory was so popular
was because Gmail’s offering was simply

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.490
laughibly absurd.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:11.110
At the time, Yahoo and Hotmail offered 2MB
of storage and 5MB of storage respectively.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:17.270
So, when Google came out with 1 GB of storage
on April Fools Day, people assumed that it

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.820
was surely a joke.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:24.680
But, April Fools Day came and left and Gmail
was still operational.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:29.480
This definitely caught people by surprise,
but Google was just getting started.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.709
In 2005, they would increase the storage to
two GB, and in 2007, they would increase the

00:02:34.709 --> 00:02:36.540
storage to four GB.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:41.879
Very quickly, Gmail went from being a joke
to being the most desirable email client.

00:02:41.879 --> 00:02:46.099
I don’t think I have to tell you the horrors
of trying to ensure that your entire email

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:49.080
archive is less than 2 or 5 MB.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.459
But, storage was just the first of many advantages
of Gmail.

00:02:53.459 --> 00:02:58.659
Gmail also boasted the best spam filters,
the best UI, convenient grouping, and ability

00:02:58.659 --> 00:03:00.760
to actually search through your email.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.489
Yes, the other popular services didn’t even
let you search.

00:03:04.489 --> 00:03:10.031
But, while everyone wanted Gmail, not very
many people could actually get it and this

00:03:10.031 --> 00:03:13.200
brings us to the second appeal of Gmail which
was scarcity.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.510
Here’s the thing, offering these crazy storage
spaces that were magnitudes above the competition

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.900
turned out to be extremely hard.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:24.970
So, they had to heavily limit the number of
users that used the service.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:29.900
For most companies, this would’ve been a
major scaling bottleneck, but Google actually

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:33.730
made it an advantage by making Gmail invite
only.

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:37.610
People could only open a gmail account if
they were invited by someone who already had

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:38.610
a gmail account.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:43.239
So, if you had a Gmail account, you were very
much the cool kid in the friend group.

00:03:43.239 --> 00:03:47.090
It wasn’t till nearly 3 years after the
initial launch that Google made Gmail available

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:48.340
to everyone.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:53.310
By this point, most people who didn’t yet
have a Gmail account were dying to switch,

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:56.260
so Gmail’s marketshare continued skyrocketing.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:59.390
It wasn’t just everyday people who craved
Gmail either.

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:04.780
Google was very much getting schools and institutions
on board as well, once again thanks to their

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:05.780
features.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:10.459
For institutions, Google offered custom domain
names, zero scheduled downtime for maintenance,

00:04:10.459 --> 00:04:17.009
30 GB of storage up to unlimited storage based
on your plan, and 24/7 phone and email support.

00:04:17.009 --> 00:04:21.640
Once again, Google blew out the competition
out of the water, so switching was a no brianer.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:26.630
But, this was basically the end of the era
of voluntary Gmail adoption.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:31.240
While Gmail was indeed the superior choice,
much of the adoption that followed in the

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:33.700
2010s was forced adoption.

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:38.501
FORCED ADOPTION:
For people who had already bought into the

00:04:38.501 --> 00:04:43.820
Google ecosystem, Google’s strategies throughout
the 2010s probably seemed completely harmless.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:48.750
But, for the people who didn’t buy into
their ecosystem, well it was definitely a

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:50.350
pain in the neck.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:54.070
What Google started doing was merging all
of their platforms and requiring that you

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:56.479
have a single Google account for all of them.

00:04:56.479 --> 00:05:01.480
This probably doesn’t seem like a big deal
today, but back in the day, people got pretty

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:02.980
heated about this.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:05.430
And likely the biggest culprit was Google+.

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:10.810
If you’re not familiar with Google+, I don’t
blame you because it ended up being a massive

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:11.810
failure.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:15.570
Google+ was basically Google’s attempt at
growing their own social media platform and

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:17.370
competing against Facebook.

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:22.220
It seems harmless at first glance, but the
problem was that instead of enticing people

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:27.240
to join Google+ because of its features, Google
forced Google+ down people’s throats.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:32.590
For example, in 2011, Google modified the
Gmail account creation process such that you

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:36.919
had to create a Google+ profile, and this
was the case up until 2014.

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:41.940
I think you can see why this would be annoying,
but this wasn’t even close to being their

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.530
worst infraction.

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:47.110
Likely their worst infraction was their policy
regarding YouTube comments.

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:52.500
In 2013, YouTube mandated that people must
have Google+ accounts if they wanted to leave

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:53.500
comments.

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:58.230
For obvious reasons, people were enraged and
even YouTube’s cofounder Jawed Karim would

00:05:58.230 --> 00:05:59.230
call out google.

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:04.380
Jawed would post quote “Why the f do I need
a Google+ account to comment on a video?”

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:10.280
Despite all the backlash, Google wouldn’t
untie YouTube and Google+ up until 2015.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:15.710
While Google+ was no doubt the worst offender,
the same fundamental argument was applicable

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:17.630
to Gmail accounts as well.

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:21.650
Google made it so that you needed a Gmail
account if you wanted to do anything else

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:22.789
on Google.

00:06:22.789 --> 00:06:24.100
Do you want to use Google Drive?

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:26.060
Well, you’re gonna need a Gmail.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:27.790
Do you want to a YouTube account?

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:29.660
Well, you’re gonna need a Gmail.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:32.310
Do you want to download apps from the play
store?

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:34.479
Well, you’re gonna need a Gmail.

00:06:34.479 --> 00:06:39.560
The funniest part was that this monopoly extended
outside of Google’s services as well.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:43.150
I’m sure you’re all familiar with the
“login with Google” feature.

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:48.699
While this feature is on doubt super convenient,
it just further strengthened the Google monopoly

00:06:48.699 --> 00:06:52.760
and forced more people to get Gmails even
if their use case had nothing to do with using

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:54.340
Gmail for email.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:58.590
A similar case could be made for the education
side of Gmail as well.

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:02.930
If universities wanted to run Google Ads or
roll out schoolwide Google calendars, they

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:07.630
needed a Google account, and they needed all
their students to have Google accounts as

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:08.630
well.

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:13.319
Now, technically, many Google features are
available without having an account, but we

00:07:13.319 --> 00:07:17.430
all know that the services are way better
if you have an account.

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:21.190
Trying to use Google’s services without
an account is like trying to use airpods with

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:22.810
a Windows computer.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:26.349
It works, but it’s not all that great.

00:07:26.349 --> 00:07:32.510
THE TURNING TIDE:
All of this brings us to where we are today.

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:37.629
Google has become one of the most dominant
email clients in the world by leveraging features,

00:07:37.629 --> 00:07:42.580
scarcity, and forced adoption, but their dominance
is slowly started to fade away.

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:44.590
So, what’s happening?

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:46.410
Well the answer is pretty simple.

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:48.539
Google is running out of trump cards.

00:07:48.539 --> 00:07:50.009
Let’s start with features.

00:07:50.009 --> 00:07:54.289
One of Gmail’s original selling features
was their massive storage, but the competition

00:07:54.289 --> 00:07:58.069
has not only caught up, they’ve actually
overtaken Google.

00:07:58.069 --> 00:07:59.900
Take Outlook for example.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:05.380
Outlook offers 15 GB of storage just for their
email alone, and you get an additional 5 GB

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:06.380
through OneDrive.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:10.699
So, Gmail is by no means leagues ahead of
the competition anymore.

00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:15.220
Moving onto Gmail’s second selling factor
of scarcity, well no one really thinks that

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:17.639
having Google account is cool anymore.

00:08:17.639 --> 00:08:22.080
It’s basically just the norm and everyone
already has one.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:26.009
This also makes it extremely difficult for
Google to force more adoption because there’s

00:08:26.009 --> 00:08:28.190
really no one left to convince.

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:32.150
Something else that we should mention is that
Google was never really the ideal choice for

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:37.390
students or professionals because you can’t
leverage Microsoft office as much.

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:41.649
The reason that many schools and universities
embraced Google docs, slides, and sheets is

00:08:41.649 --> 00:08:44.519
because they made collaboration extremely
convenient.

00:08:44.519 --> 00:08:48.810
But, if you put the convenience and collaboration
aspects aside, there’s no question that

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:51.580
Microsoft office is the superior service.

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:55.950
And over the past 10 years, Microsoft has
very much made strides with their own online

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:58.470
office products and collaboration features.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:03.910
Microsoft Office online is actually free,
but that doesn’t even matter to institutions

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:07.800
because they usually already have a bunch
of office 365 subscriptions anyway.

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.280
So, many institutions are switching back to
Outlook to create synergies between email,

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:14.950
cloud storage, and Microsoft Office.

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:19.519
We should also mention that while schools
and universities very much embraced Google,

00:09:19.519 --> 00:09:21.100
companies never did.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:27.060
In fact, nearly 196,000 companies in the US
use outlook while only 4,400 use Gmail.

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:30.940
Another catalyst that has stolen market share
away from Gmail is Apple’s recent Mail Privacy

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:32.329
Protection program.

00:09:32.329 --> 00:09:36.890
This program makes it harder for email marketers
to track down your IP address and your mail

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:37.890
activity.

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:41.670
And in age where everyone is worried about
juggernauts like Google and Facebook collecting

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:46.850
your data, it’s not surprising that people
are taking every chance to project themselves.

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:50.950
This is made even easier by the fact that
you don’t have to do anything to take advantage

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:51.950
of this program.

00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:56.150
In fact, it would be easier to take advantage
of this program than not take advantage of

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:57.150
it.

00:09:57.150 --> 00:10:00.510
All you have to do is use the inbuilt mail
app on iPhones instead of downloading the

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:01.910
Gmail app.

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:06.570
And while people have been weary of the iPhone
email app for years, it seems like this new

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:11.040
feature is very much pushing people towards
it, and we can see this in the market share

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:12.130
data.

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:17.400
In September of 2021, Apple had 50% market
share, but today they have nearly 60% market

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:18.400
share.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:23.040
And I suspect that this will only grow with
time as people become more aware of the importance

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:24.800
of data protection.

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:31.260
THE FUTURE OF GMAIL:
At the end of the day, Gmail is by no means

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:33.570
dying or becoming irrelevant.

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:37.190
You very much still need a Gmail account to
surf the internet freely.

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:42.890
But it does seem like people are using other
services for email more and more frequently.

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:46.220
Some of the surface levels reasons for this
are that competing email clients have caught

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:50.350
up in terms of features, they have better
integration with Microsoft Office, and Google

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:52.390
has basically just reached market saturation.

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:58.290
But, if we look beyond that, it appears that
we’re seeing a larger trend towards privacy.

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:01.940
People are more aware than ever how these
companies are able to offer their services

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:04.550
for free and how they really make money.

00:11:04.550 --> 00:11:09.050
For example, here’s all the data the Google
is able to collect just off of Gmail.

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:12.780
It appears that Apple has caught onto this
consumer awareness and they’re very much

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:14.600
framing their business with this mind.

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:19.150
They’re positioning themselves as the big
tech company that doesn’t collect data.

00:11:19.150 --> 00:11:23.170
This can be seen with their “Ask Not To
Track” feature and their mail privacy protection

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:24.170
program.

00:11:24.170 --> 00:11:27.090
It’s not just Apple who’s jumping onto
this trend either.

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:31.389
In the messaging space, we have Signal and
in the social media space, we have BeReal.

00:11:31.389 --> 00:11:37.500
While these services offer completely different
functionality, their core appeal is the identical:

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:40.200
Erasing the bad aspects of big tech.

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:45.560
And given that Google already doesn’t have
the much room to grow, I suspect that Gmail’s

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:51.030
popularity will simply continue to trend downwards,
but that’s just what I think.

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:53.300
